'''Teruhito''' ({{nihongo|Teruhito-shinnō|輝仁親王}}; born 3 November 2010) is a member of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken]] and the third child and second son of Emperor Masahito and Empress Michiko. He is currently second in the line of succession to the Chrysanthemum Throne, following his elder brother, [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]. Unlike his brother's focus on heavy industry and athletics, Teruhito is recognized for his artistic patronage and prodigious talent as a classical musician, earning him the popular moniker "The Prince of Strings."

==Early life and education==
Prince Teruhito was born in 2010 at the Imperial Household Hospital in [[Okami]]. His personal name, Teruhito (using the character for "Radiance"), was chosen by his father to symbolize a hope for a bright and cultured future for the nation. As a child, he was described as quiet and introspective, contrasting with the boisterous energy of his elder brother Tomohito.

He attended the Gakushūin Primary School, where he excelled in literature and arts. He is currently enrolled at the Gakushūin Boys' Senior High School in Okami. While he maintains high grades in standard subjects, his academic focus has increasingly shifted toward music theory and composition. In 2024, he caused a minor stir in the conservative Imperial Household Agency by petitioning to take elective courses at the Okami National Conservatory of Music alongside his regular high school studies, a request that was ultimately granted by the Emperor.

==Musical pursuits==
[[File:Prince_Teruhito_Cello.png|thumb|left|200px|Prince Teruhito performing for an event at the palace.]]
Teruhito's interest in music began at the age of four when he was given a toy violin by his grandmother. He quickly outgrew the toy and began formal lessons on the violin and the cello. By the age of ten, he was performing private recitals for visiting dignitaries.

His primary instrument is the cello, though he is also proficient on the piano and the ''koto'', a traditional Sanese zither. Critics have noted that his playing style is characterized by deep emotional resonance and technical precision. In 2023, he made his public debut at a charity gala for the Red Cross, performing Bach's Cello Suite No. 1. The performance was broadcast nationally and was praised for its somber maturity.

The Prince sees music as a vital diplomatic tool. He has expressed a desire to revive the "Diplomacy of Strings," a historical concept from the Okaiken court where cultural exchange was used to soften the nation's militaristic image. He is an avid composer and has written several short pieces for chamber orchestras, one of which, titled ''Wind over Toyozawa'', was performed by the Imperial Court Orchestra during the Emperor's birthday celebrations in 2024.

==Public image==
[[File:Prince Terhuito Portrait.png|thumb|right|240px|A photo of Prince Teruhito]]
In the media narrative of the Imperial family, Teruhito is often portrayed as the "Soft Power" complement to Crown Prince Tomohito's "Hard Power." While Tomohito represents the physical strength and industrial might of Okaiken (the "Iron"), Teruhito represents its artistic soul and refinement (the "Strings"). This dynamic is popular among the public, who view the brothers as a balanced pair capable of leading the nation into a new era. Despite his youth, Teruhito carries himself with a solemn dignity and is rarely seen without a book or a musical score in hand during public engagements.

